#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "d6a54cb2-ea48-57b1-bf2d-7aac09915159" :question/math-academy-id "e-8595" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}}⟨0, t, 4⟩,; N(t)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}}⟨0, 4,-t⟩$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t) &= T(t) \\cdot N(t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}}⟨0, t, 4⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}}⟨0, 4,-t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t^{2} + 16}i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 0 & t\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 4\\begin{vmatrix}-t \\\\ = \\frac{1}{t^{2} + 16}[t\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- ti - 0\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}- tj + 0 \\mid t \\\\\n&= 0\\end{vmatrix}4k] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t^{2} + 16}⟨(-t^{2} - 16), - (0 - 0), (0 - 0)⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t^{2} + 16}⟨ - (t^{2} + 16), 0, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "b2a775f5-4a27-5cba-931f-2a45db305152" :question/math-academy-id "e-8596" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point $t=\\pi$, given that\n\n$T(π)|= \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3, 4⟩,; N(π)|= ⟨1, 0, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(π) &= T(π) \\cdot N(π) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨1, 0, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ = \\frac{1}{5}[- 3\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}0i - 0\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0j + 0 \\mid - 3 \\\\\n&= 1\\end{vmatrix}0k] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}⟨(0 - 0), - (0 - 4), (0 - (-3))⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}⟨0, 4, 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "e805dcee-247b-5aa3-9856-f7bd48bd1a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-8597" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$T(t)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\sin 4t, 2, \\cos 4t⟩$.\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t)\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t)$. To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(t) &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin 4t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos 4t)⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨4\\cos 4t, 0,-4\\sin 4t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\cos 4t, 0,-\\sin 4t⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(t)‖ &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(4\\cos 4t)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-4\\sin 4t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} 4t + 16\\sin^{2} 4t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16(\\cos^{2} 4t + \\sin^{2} 4t)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) &= (T^{′}(t))/(‖T^{′}(t)‖) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\cos 4t, 0,-\\sin 4t⟩)/((\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})) \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos 4t, 0,-\\sin 4t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t) &= T(t) \\cdot N(t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\sin 4t, 2, \\cos 4t⟩ \\cdot ⟨\\cos 4t, 0,-\\sin 4t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ \\sin 4t & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cos 4t \\\\\n&= \\cos 4t \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sin 4t \\\\ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[2\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos 4t \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}- \\sin 4ti - \\sin 4t\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos 4t \\\\ \\cos 4t\\end{vmatrix}- \\sin 4tj + \\sin 4t \\mid 2 \\\\\n&= \\cos 4t\\end{vmatrix}0k] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨ - 2\\sin 4t - 0, - (-\\sin^{2} 4t - \\cos^{2} 4t), 0 - 2\\cos 4t⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨ - 2\\sin 4t, 1,-2\\cos 4t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6ff39e-73f7-521c-84d8-7b27af6185c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2\\sin t, 2\\cos t, \\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166153} {:answer/id #uuid "da27ebe0-018f-59a3-8731-9a15ffc67a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\sin t, \\cos t, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166154} {:answer/id #uuid "0f76f5a7-9c09-5ec1-86e2-34d76c5f1f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨ - \\sin t,-\\cos t, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166155} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed35365-5029-59